        "title": "Trees and Forests - Structure, Succession, and Old Growth",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "HEIGHT (m)\n  55+│ EMERGENT LAYER\n     │  (occasional giants above canopy)\n     │  Giant sequoia, dipterocarp emergents, rainforest emergents\n  45 │\n     │ CANOPY LAYER\n  30 │  Main continuous leaf surface; intercepts 70-95% of light\n     │  Species: climate-dependent (oak/beech/maple in temperate;\n     │  dipterocarps/figs in tropical; spruce/fir in boreal)\n  15 │\n     │ UNDERSTORY LAYER\n     │  Shade-tolerant small trees, midcanopy (dogwood, serviceberry)\n  8  │  Wait decades for canopy gap to become canopy tree\n     │\n  3  │ SHRUB LAYER\n     │  Woody shrubs (mountain laurel, rhoddy, elderberry)\n     │  Often responds dramatically to canopy gaps\n  0.5│\n     │ HERB LAYER\n     │  Ferns, wildflowers, sedges, grass, saplings\n     │  Spring ephemeral strategy: flower BEFORE canopy leafout\n   ──┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  0.0  FOREST FLOOR\n     ─── Litter, moss, fungi, decomposing wood, mineral soil ──────\n     ─── ROOT SYSTEMS + MYCORRHIZAL NETWORKS (extend below ground)──\n```"
    },
